const NES_EMU_FDS_HTML = "<section class=\"gbemu-hero\"><div class=\"gbemu-hero-num\"><b>Disk System</b><span>build specification</span></div><div class=\"gbemu-hero-txt\"><h3>Disk System: how to build it, exactly as our core runs it</h3><p>The Famicom Disk System: the RAM adapter, its registers and timer interrupt, the disk transfer model, and disk changes. Every statement says how it is known. This is one area of the build specification; the index lists all eight.</p></div></section>\n\n<section class=\"sect nesemu-chapter\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds\">The Famicom Disk System</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s1\">1. What the implementer receives and what the user supplies</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s2\">2. The host's loading sequence (<code>web/core.js</code> <code>loadDiskSystem</code>)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s3\">3. The adapter's memory map</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s4\">4. The register map, bit by bit</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s5\">5. The timer interrupt</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s6\">6. The interrupt line</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s7\">7. The drive as a transport</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s8\">8. Writing</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s9\">9. The drive firmware's role</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s10\">10. Disks: insert, eject, side swap</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s11\">11. Region</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s12\">12. Registered choices</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s13\">13. Not established, and open questions</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s14\">14. The disk-identity check</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds-s15\">15. Glossary</a></nav>\n<p>This chapter specifies the Famicom Disk System as the core models it: the RAM adapter that sits in the cartridge slot, its register window at <code>$4020</code>-<code>$4033</code>, the timer interrupt, and the drive, which is a <em>transport</em> with a head, a motor and a fixed data rate. The adapter's wavetable sound channel (<code>$4040</code>-<code>$4092</code>) is specified in <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">the expansion audio chapter</a> and is only referred to here.</p>\n<p>Read this chapter with three facts in mind:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>The Disk System is not a cartridge.</b> Nothing in the slot holds a program. The adapter holds 32 KB of RAM, 8 KB of character RAM and the drive's own 8 KB firmware. The program arrives <em>from the disk</em>, loaded by that firmware.</li><li><b>The drive firmware is part of the machine under test, and it is not ours.</b> It is supplied by the user from their own hardware. It is never committed to this tree, never fetched, and never patched by the core. A run that involves the Disk System names the firmware revision it used.</li><li><b>The container is not the disk.</b> A <code>.fds</code> side stores decoded block contents back to back. It has no gaps, no checksums and no surface markers. The firmware expects all three, so the core rebuilds a disk <em>surface</em> from the container as the head moves. Most of this chapter is about that rebuild.</li></ol>\n<p>The container world calls this machine <b>mapper 20</b>. That number means \"not a cartridge at all\". A core that sees mapper 20 switches the cartridge bus to the adapter's memory map below.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s1\">1. What the implementer receives and what the user supplies</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>input</th><th>size</th><th>where it comes from</th><th>committed?</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>drive firmware</td><td>exactly 8,192 bytes</td><td>the user's own drive; the host rejects any other size</td><td><b>never</b></td><td>hardware rule: the firmware is 8 KB, mapped <code>$E000</code>-<code>$FFFF</code></td></tr><tr><td>disk image (<code>.fds</code>)</td><td>a whole number of 65,500-byte sides, after an optional 16-byte header is removed</td><td>the user's own disks</td><td><b>never</b></td><td>hardware rule: the container definition</td></tr><tr><td>adapter RAM</td><td>32,768 bytes</td><td>allocated and zero-filled by the host</td><td>n/a</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td>character RAM</td><td>8,192 bytes</td><td>allocated and zero-filled by the host</td><td>n/a</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The pin.</b> The test tools name and hash the Disk System inputs, which stay outside the tree, and refuse to run if they have moved. Three drive firmware images are pinned, all 8,192 bytes and all distinct:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>SHA-256 (prefix)</th><th>standing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>nintendo-rev1</code></td><td><code>99c18490…</code></td><td>the drive the tests run on</td></tr><tr><td><code>nintendo-orig</code></td><td><code>fdc1a76e…</code></td><td>the other Nintendo drive. By measurement it is the only <em>independent</em> witness: it differs from rev1 by 39.7% of its bytes</td></tr><tr><td><code>sharp-twin</code></td><td><code>a0a9d57c…</code></td><td>a different manufacturer's drive. It differs from rev1 by only 9.5% of its bytes, so it counts as a second machine, never a third witness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>No firmware or disk bytes are in this chapter.</b> Nothing can run a disk until the user supplies a firmware image.</p>\n<p><b>The tests.</b> Two disk images from the public test corpus are test-board rows: <code>fdsirqtests.fds</code> and <code>fdsirqtestsV7_patched.fds</code>. Each prints 21 sub-tests on screen, marking each O or X, and they decide the timer interrupt and the <code>$4023</code> decode (section 5). Both pass on this core, with the <code>nintendo-rev1</code> drive firmware. No other Disk System behaviour has a self-reporting test. Everything else in this chapter states its own basis.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s2\">2. The host's loading sequence (<code>web/core.js</code> <code>loadDiskSystem</code>)</h4>\n<p>An implementer gets the host, so this section describes what the host does <em>to</em> the core. The core must accept this exact call order.</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Check the firmware.</b> If it is not exactly 8,192 bytes, refuse.</li><li><b>Remove the fwNES header.</b> If the image is at least 16 bytes long and begins with the four bytes <code>F</code>, <code>D</code>, <code>S</code>, <code>$1A</code>, drop its first 16 bytes. Otherwise use the image as it is. Some containers carry this header and some do not.</li><li><b>Check the length.</b> If what remains is empty or is not a multiple of 65,500, refuse (\"not a whole number of sides\"). The core never sees a partial side.</li><li>Allocate the context, an 8,192-byte program area (filled with the firmware), an 8,192-byte character RAM area (zeroed), a 32,768-byte adapter RAM area (zeroed), and the disk buffer (filled with the sides). Copy the disk into linear memory once. <b>The core does not copy it again.</b> It keeps a pointer, and drive writes go into this buffer.</li><li><b>Region.</b> If the caller asked for automatic region, use NTSC. A disk carries no region, and the drive is Japanese. If a caller explicitly passes another region, the core runs that region with the same cycle counts. Nothing adjusts the drive's timing per region (every drive figure below is in CPU cycles).</li><li><code>nes_init</code>, then the audio divider, then <code>nes_set_cart</code> with program = firmware (8,192), character = the RAM area (8,192, flagged as RAM), work RAM = 32,768, <b>mapper 20</b>, nametable source = PPU A11 (wiring code <code>CIRAM_A11</code> = 0, which the container world calls \"horizontal mirroring\" although it stacks the nametables in the <em>vertical arrangement</em>; see <a href=\"#nesemu-40-cartridge\">40-CARTRIDGE.md</a>), battery 0, submapper 0.</li><li><code>nes_set_disk(pointer, length)</code>. This <b>must precede</b> <code>nes_power_on</code>, because the drive's power-on keeps the side count that <code>nes_set_disk</code> computed.</li><li><code>nes_power_on</code>.</li></ol>\n<p><b>The host does not insert the disk.</b> After power-on the core reports a disk in the drive, side 0 (section 10). The host's swap control, <code>FdsDrive</code> in <code>web/shell.js</code>, then ejects it and inserts it again a few frames later. Section 10 gives that timed policy and its two tuned constants.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s3\">3. The adapter's memory map</h4>\n<p>These rows apply when the cartridge is mapper 20. They replace the generic cartridge decode.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>CPU range</th><th>size</th><th>what is there</th><th>reads</th><th>writes</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4020</code>-<code>$4092</code></td><td>115 B</td><td>the adapter's register window (sections 4-5; <code>$4040</code>-<code>$4092</code> is audio, see <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a>)</td><td>per register; an address no register answers returns open bus</td><td>per register; others ignored</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4093</code>-<code>$5FFF</code></td><td></td><td>nothing</td><td>open bus</td><td>ignored</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$6000</code>-<code>$DFFF</code></td><td>32 KB</td><td><b>adapter RAM</b>, one contiguous unbanked window. The index is (address − <code>$6000</code>) modulo the RAM size</td><td>RAM</td><td>RAM</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$E000</code>-<code>$FFFF</code></td><td>8 KB</td><td><b>drive firmware</b>, read-only. The index is (address − <code>$E000</code>) modulo the firmware size</td><td>firmware</td><td>ignored</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Consequences:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>$8000</code>-<code>$DFFF</code>, which holds program firmware on every other board, is <b>RAM</b> here. It is the upper 24 KB of the same 32 KB window.</li><li><b>The three vectors (<code>$FFFA</code>, <code>$FFFC</code>, <code>$FFFE</code>) come from the drive firmware.</b> The firmware's handlers then jump through vectors it keeps in adapter RAM. That indirection is the firmware's own code, and the machine models none of it.</li><li><b>Character RAM</b> is a plain 8 KB, unbanked and writable. The adapter has no character banking.</li><li><b>Nametable mirroring</b> is set by <code>$4025</code> bit 3 (section 4). Until the program first writes <code>$4025</code>, it is whatever the host passed: PPU A11 (<code>CIRAM_A11</code>, \"horizontal mirroring\", the vertical arrangement). See <a href=\"#nesemu-20-ppu\">the picture unit chapter</a> for what each source means.</li><li><b>Adapter RAM at power-on</b> is whatever the host placed there. The host zero-fills it, and the core's power-on does not touch it.</li><li><b>The decode and the clock are keyed differently.</b> The memory map and registers above apply whenever the mapper number is 20. The drive's per-cycle clock (watchdog, transport, timer, sound) and the watchdog's row count (4.8) run only when a disk buffer has been set (<code>nes_set_disk</code> with a non-null pointer). With mapper 20 and no disk, the registers answer but nothing ticks. The disk-identity check (section 14) is keyed to the mapper number, not to the disk.</li><li><b>A debugging peek never goes through the register read path.</b> <code>nes_peek</code> (and the trace writer) returns open bus for all of <code>$4020</code>-<code>$4092</code> on mapper 20, because reading <code>$4030</code> or <code>$4031</code> has side effects (section 6).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s4\">4. The register map, bit by bit</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.1 Write registers</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>address</th><th>name</th><th>bits</th><th>effect</th><th>gated by <code>$4023</code> bit 0?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4020</code></td><td>timer reload, low</td><td>7-0</td><td>low byte of the 16-bit reload value. The running counter is not touched</td><td><b>no</b> (confirmed by fdsirqtests \"Set RelVal DskRg Off\")</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4021</code></td><td>timer reload, high</td><td>7-0</td><td>high byte of the reload value. The running counter is not touched</td><td><b>no</b> (confirmed by the same sub-test)</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4022</code></td><td>timer control</td><td>see 4.2</td><td>arms or disarms the timer interrupt</td><td><b>yes</b>, when <code>FDS_IO_DECODE_GATE</code> = 1 (confirmed by fdsirqtests \"No Disk Reg = No IRQ\", \"Enbl DiskR after 4022\")</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4023</code></td><td>master I/O enable</td><td>see 4.3</td><td></td><td>never, or nothing could turn the rest back on</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4024</code></td><td>write data</td><td>7-0</td><td>latched as the byte the drive writes in the next write-mode byte period. A <b>latch, not a queue</b>: if the CPU is late, the drive writes the previous value again. With <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 0 writing it does <b>not</b> clear byte-ready; with the default 1 it does (4.8)</td><td><b>yes</b> with <code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code> = 1, the default (4.8); with 0, <b>no</b> (not established on hardware; no test measures it)</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4025</code></td><td>drive control</td><td>see 4.4</td><td></td><td>as <code>$4024</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>$4026</code></td><td>external connector output</td><td>7-0</td><td>ignored. Nothing is attached</td><td>as <code>$4024</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>$4027</code>-<code>$403F</code></td><td></td><td></td><td>ignored</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>When <code>$4023</code> bit 0 is clear, a write to <code>$4022</code> is <b>dropped completely</b>. Neither the repeat bit nor the enable bit is stored. Turning bit 0 back on later does not bring that write back. With <code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code> = 1 (the default) the same holds for <code>$4024</code>, <code>$4025</code> and <code>$4026</code> (4.8).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.2 <code>$4022</code>, timer control</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td><b>repeat</b>: 1 = reload and keep counting after an interrupt, 0 = disarm after one interrupt</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><b>enable</b>: on a write with this bit 1, the counter is loaded from the reload value immediately. On a write with this bit 0, the timer is disarmed, its pending flag is cleared, <b>and the shared cartridge interrupt line is dropped</b> (section 6)</td></tr><tr><td>2-7</td><td>ignored</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.3 <code>$4023</code>, master I/O enable</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td><b>disk I/O enable.</b> 1 = the drive's registers are decoded and the byte-transfer interrupt may assert. A write with this bit 0 also disarms the timer (enable ← 0), clears its pending flag, and drops the shared interrupt line; with the 4.8 defaults it does more, in the order given in 4.8 (<em>The <code>$4023</code> write</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><b>sound enable.</b> It gates the wavetable channel; see <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a></td></tr><tr><td>2-7</td><td>ignored</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Power-on value: both bits 0. With I/O disabled, the byte interrupt never asserts, the timer cannot be armed, and <code>$4022</code> writes are dropped. The transport still turns, though: <code>$4023</code> bit 0 does not stop the head.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.4 <code>$4025</code>, drive control</h5>\n<p>Every write applies the steps below, in this order.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>name in this core</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>motor</td><td>1 = motor on. A <b>0</b> stops the drive: see <em>stop</em> in section 7.1. A <b>0→1</b> change starts spin-up: the spin-up counter is set to <code>FDS_SPINUP_CYCLES</code></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>transfer reset</td><td>1 = hold the head at the start of the side (section 7.2). A <b>1→0</b> change <em>releases</em> it, and the scan begins: see <em>release</em> in section 7.2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>direction</td><td>1 = read, 0 = write. A change <b>from read to write</b> clears the \"past the last block\" state and the current block's remaining length, and arms the write path: a start mark is expected next, no checksum is in progress, and the block start is the current head (section 8)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>mirroring</td><td>0 = vertical (nametable select from PPU A10, wiring code <code>CIRAM_A10</code>: the horizontal arrangement), 1 = horizontal (from PPU A11, wiring code <code>CIRAM_A11</code>: the vertical arrangement). Applied on every write. The words \"vertical\" and \"horizontal\" here are the mirroring names of <a href=\"#nesemu-40-cartridge\">40-CARTRIDGE.md</a>, not the arrangement</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>CRC control</td><td>write mode only: 1 = the drive is appending the block's checksum, so the block the CPU was writing is over (section 8). The read path never consults it</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td></td><td>ignored</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>transfer start</td><td>write mode only: 0 = the head writes gap; 1 = the transfer runs, and the first non-zero byte the CPU then supplies is the block's start mark (section 8). The read path never consults it</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>byte-transfer interrupt enable</td><td>1 = a byte period raises the interrupt. With <code>FDS_BYTE_IRQ_GATE</code> = 1, a write with this bit <b>0</b> drops the line and then re-asserts it if the timer flag or the watchdog flag (4.8) is set at that moment (section 6)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Order of effects within one taken <code>$4025</code> write</b> (a write the decode gate of 4.8 drops has none of them):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Store <code>r4025</code> (4.8) and bits 0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, handling the direction change as described in the bit 2 row.</li><li>Apply the bit 7 gate (the bit 7 row).</li><li>Set mirroring from bit 3.</li><li>If the transfer reset went 1→0 in this write, <em>release</em> (7.2).</li><li>If the motor bit is 0, <em>stop</em> (7.1). Otherwise, if the motor bit went 0→1 in this write, set the spin-up counter to <code>FDS_SPINUP_CYCLES</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>A single write that releases the reset and clears the motor therefore ends stopped.</p>\n<p><b>Bits 4 and 6 (<code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 1).</b> Bit 4 is the checksum control and bit 6 the transfer start. The read path does not depend on either bit.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The published Disk System register notes and other emulators' source for the drive agree on the bit assignment. Measured in the drive firmware's own write of a block (Metroid registering a name), from a log of <code>$4025</code> writes: it writes <code>$21</code> (write mode, bit 6 clear), then <code>$61</code> and <code>$E1</code> (bit 6 set), hands over zeros, the <code>$80</code> start mark and the block, then <code>$F1</code> (bit 4 set) for the checksum, then <code>$25</code> (back to read). Rival <code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 0: bit 6 is the checksum control and bit 4 is ignored; with it every period from <code>$61</code> on reads \"the block is over\", nothing is stored, and Metroid and Zelda show ERR 24 after registering a name.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.5 Read registers</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>address</th><th>name</th><th>value</th><th>read side effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4030</code></td><td>disk status</td><td>see 4.6</td><td>clears the <b>timer</b> flag and drops the shared interrupt line; with <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 1 (the default) it also clears byte-ready and the watchdog flag (4.8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4031</code></td><td>read data</td><td>the last byte the transport delivered</td><td>clears <b>byte-ready</b> and drops the shared interrupt line</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4032</code></td><td>drive status</td><td>see 4.7</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4033</code></td><td>external connector input</td><td><code>$80</code> OR (open bus AND <code>$7F</code>); with <code>NES_R18_FDS_4033_MOTOR</code> = 1 (the default) bit 7 follows <code>$4025</code> bit 1 (4.8)</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4040</code>-<code>$407F</code>, <code>$4090</code>-<code>$4092</code></td><td>audio</td><td>see <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>every other address in <code>$4020</code>-<code>$4092</code></td><td></td><td>open bus</td><td>none</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.6 <code>$4030</code>, disk status</h5>\n<p>The bits as below hold with the 4.8 switches at 0; with their defaults, bit 7 is byte-ready, bit 1 the watchdog flag and bit 3 the arrangement bit (4.8).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>meaning</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>timer interrupt flag pending</td><td>test: fdsirqtests</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><b>byte-ready</b>: the transport delivered a byte (read) or freed the shift register (write) since the last <code>$4031</code> read</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>open bus</td><td>hardware rule: undriven bits read as open bus, as at every other partly driven register in this core</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>open bus</td><td>as above</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>CRC error. <b>Always 0</b></td><td>declared limit: the container discarded the real checksums, so there is nothing to check against. A corrupt disk reads as good</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>open bus</td><td>as above</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>end of head: the head has run off the end of the side</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><b>always 1</b> (read/write enable)</td><td>not established as a dynamic signal. With <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 0 the bit is constant 1; the default 1 makes it byte-ready (4.8)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>What reading <code>$4030</code> clears, exactly</b> (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 0; the default also clears byte-ready and the watchdog flag, 4.8). It clears the timer flag (bit 0). It drops the shared interrupt line, so a pending <em>byte</em> interrupt is acknowledged as well. It does <b>not</b> clear byte-ready (bit 1), and it does <b>not</b> clear end-of-head (bit 6). Byte-ready stays set until <code>$4031</code> is read.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example (the 4.8 switches at 0): LDA $4030 (absolute) with the timer flag set,\na byte ready, head on the surface.  The last value on the bus before the read is the operand\nhigh byte, $40, so the open-bus bits 2, 3 and 5 read 0.\n  value = $80 | $02 | $01 | ($40 &amp; $2C) = $83\nAfter the read: timer flag 0, byte-ready still 1, interrupt line released.</code></pre>\n<p>The same read with the 4.8 switches at their defaults:</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example (defaults): LDA $4030 (absolute) with the timer flag set, a byte\nready, no watchdog flag, head on the surface, r4025 = $25 (motor on, read, bit 3\nclear), open bus $40.\n  value = $80 (byte-ready, bit 7) | $01 (timer) | ($25 &amp; $08) | ($40 &amp; $24) = $81\nAfter the read: timer flag 0, byte-ready 0, watchdog flag 0, interrupt line released.</code></pre>\n<p>A default-switch example with bit 3 set is in 4.8.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.7 <code>$4032</code>, drive status</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>meaning (1 =)</th><th>composed from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td><b>no disk</b></td><td>the drive is empty</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><b>not ready</b></td><td>with <code>FDS_READY_POLARITY</code> = 1, the OR of: drive empty; motor bit 0; spin-up still running; and, when <code>FDS_READY_ON_RESET</code> = 1, the transfer-reset bit set</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><b>write-protected</b></td><td>the write-protect state, <b>or</b> the drive is empty. An empty drive has no surface to write</td></tr><tr><td>3-7</td><td>open bus</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With the rival <code>FDS_READY_POLARITY</code> = 0, the media-and-motor term is inverted, so bit 1 becomes \"ready\". The transfer-reset term is <b>not</b> inverted, and still ORs a 1 into bit 1 while reset is held. Reading <code>$4032</code> changes nothing.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example: LDA $4032 after power-on, before the host's first eject:\ndisk in, motor off, write protect off, open bus $40.\n  bit0 = 0, bit1 = 1 (motor off), bit2 = 0, bits 3-7 = $40 &amp; $F8 = $40\n  value = $42</code></pre>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.8 The adapter's status bits, enables and DRAM watchdog</h5>\n<p>Each behaviour below is behind its own switch, default 1. Sources: NESdev wiki \"Family Computer Disk System\"; TakuikaNinja's Twin Famicom tests as reported on the NESdev forum (p=305115, t=26359). With the switches at their defaults, these rules replace those of 4.1, 4.3, 4.5 and 4.6 where they differ; the earlier sections describe each switch at 0.</p>\n<p>The core keeps <code>r4025</code>, the value of <code>$4025</code> as the adapter holds it: set by every <code>$4025</code> write that is taken, 0 at power-on.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Switch</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_4030_MIRROR</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_4033_MOTOR</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_DRAM_WATCHDOG</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_HALT_REREAD</code></td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_FDS_4030_MIRROR</code>.</b> <code>$4030</code> bit 3 = <code>r4025</code> bit 3 (the nametable arrangement), not open bus. Bits 2 and 5 stay open bus.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Measured on hardware: FDS-Mirroring-Test \"$4030.D3 R\".</p></div>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code>.</b> <code>$4030</code> bit 7 is the <b>byte transfer flag</b> (byte-ready), bit 1 is the <b>DRAM watchdog flag</b> (below), and the constant 1 on bit 7 is gone. A <code>$4030</code> read clears byte-ready (as well as the timer flag and the watchdog flag, and drops the shared line as before). A taken <code>$4024</code> write clears byte-ready and acknowledges the disk interrupt: the line drops and is re-asserted if the timer flag or the watchdog flag is set. <code>$4031</code> is unchanged.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The wiki: \"Byte transfer flag ... reset when $4024, $4031, or $4030 has been serviced\"; \"Writing to this register [$4024] acknowledges disk IRQs\". Another report says a <code>$4030</code> read does not clear the flag; the wiki is followed. Failure mode of the switch at 0: a program polling bit 7 for a byte (Tonkachi Editor) sees 1 at once.</p></div>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example (both switches on): LDA $4030 with the timer flag set, a byte\nready, r4025 = $2D (motor on, transfer reset 0, read, bit 3 set), no watchdog\nflag, open bus $40.\n  value = $80 (byte) | $01 (timer) | ($2D &amp; $08) | ($40 &amp; $24) = $89\nAfter the read: timer flag 0, byte-ready 0, watchdog flag 0, line released.</code></pre>\n<p>The example's <code>r4025</code> has motor bit 0 = 1: with it 0 the drive would be stopped and byte-ready already 0 (7.1).</p>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code>.</b> While <code>$4023</code> bit 0 is 0, writes to <code>$4024</code>, <code>$4025</code> and <code>$4026</code> are dropped (as <code>$4022</code> is with <code>FDS_IO_DECODE_GATE</code> = 1). While <code>$4023</code> bit 1 is 0, writes to <code>$4080-$408A</code> are dropped; <code>$4040-$407F</code> stay writable. Reads are never gated. A dropped write still counts in <code>nes_fds_writes</code>, and a dropped <code>$4025</code> write still sets <code>nes_fds_last4025</code> (section 10).</p>\n<p><b>The <code>$4023</code> write</b> (the switches at their defaults). Every <code>$4023</code> write is taken (it is never gated) and does, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Store bit 0 (disk I/O enable) and bit 1 (sound enable).</li><li>If bit 0 is 1, stop here.</li><li>Timer enable ← 0, timer flag ← 0, watchdog flag ← 0, and the shared interrupt line is dropped.</li><li>The adapter makes its own <code>$4025</code> = <code>$06</code> write. It is taken although bit 0 is now 0, and it runs every step of 4.4 in order: <code>r4025</code> ← <code>$06</code>; motor 0, transfer reset 1, read direction, bit 4 = 0, bit 6 = 0, bit 7 = 0; the bit 7 gate drops the line and re-asserts nothing, because step 3 cleared both flags; arrangement bit 0, so vertical mirroring (<code>CIRAM_A10</code>); the transfer reset did not go 1→0 in this write, so no <em>release</em>; motor 0, so <em>stop</em>. It does not count in <code>nes_fds_writes</code> and does not change <code>nes_fds_last4025</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>With <code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code> = 0, step 4 does not happen; with <code>NES_R18_FDS_DRAM_WATCHDOG</code> = 0 there is no watchdog flag to clear.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Measured on hardware: \"Setting $4023.0 to 0 resets the $4025 to value $06\"; \"Wavetable RAM remains writable when D1=0\", forum p=305115. Failure mode without the gate: a title that parks the adapter and writes the drive moves the drive.</p></div>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_FDS_4033_MOTOR</code>.</b> <code>$4033</code> = (<code>r4025</code> bit 1 = 0 ? <code>$80</code> : 0) OR (open bus AND <code>$7F</code>). Bit 1 of <code>$4025</code> is the wiki's \"Drive Motor Control (0: start, 1: stop)\"; this core calls it the transfer reset. With the motor stopped, bit 7 is the expansion port's, which has no pull-up: 0.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Measured on hardware: \"$4033.D7 ... observable only when motor is enabled\".</p></div>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_FDS_DRAM_WATCHDOG</code>.</b> Two counters in the adapter. Both work only while a disk buffer is set (section 3, <em>The decode and the clock are keyed differently</em>).</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>A <b>7-bit refresh-row counter</b>. It counts <b>CPU bus cycles</b>: every cycle in which the CPU itself reads or writes the bus (opcode and operand fetches, dummy reads and writes, stack pushes and pulls, and the cycles of an interrupt sequence), at <b>any</b> address. For each such cycle whose address is <b>not</b> <code>$6000-$DFFF</code>, while <code>$4023</code> bit 0 is 1, it adds 1 (wrapping from 127 to 0); when it wraps to 0 the cycle timer is set to <b>3245</b>. Not counted: the cycles of a direct memory access (the sprite DMA's reads and writes, the DMC fetch's read, and the halt, dummy and alignment cycles with their re-reads, <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a>), and debugging peeks.</li><li>A <b>12-bit cycle timer</b>, stepped once per CPU cycle as the first of the drive's per-cycle clocks (7.2): while <code>$4023</code> bit 0 is 0 it is set to 4095 and the row counter to 0; otherwise, if it is 0 it becomes 4095, the <b>watchdog flag</b> is set and the shared interrupt line is raised; else it is decremented by 1.</li></ol>\n<p>In one CPU cycle the timer's step (item 2) comes first, in the drive clock that runs before the cycle's bus access; the row count (item 1) is taken at that bus access. So the drive must be told the address of <b>every</b> CPU bus cycle, not only of the cycles that fall in the adapter's own ranges (<code>$4020</code>-<code>$5FFF</code> and <code>$E000</code>-<code>$FFFF</code>): a core that calls the drive only for its own ranges cannot count rows.</p>\n<p>At power-on both counters are 0 (7.1). The first drive clock after power-on finds <code>$4023</code> bit 0 = 0 and sets the timer to 4095; because a cycle's drive clock runs before that cycle's bus access, no <code>$4023</code> write can find the timer at its power-on 0.</p>\n<p><code>$4023</code> bit 7 is not modelled as a control: it \"cannot be changed\" and powers on set, and the interrupt is armed by it, so the watchdog is armed whenever bit 0 is 1. The flag reads on <code>$4030</code> bit 1 (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code>), and is cleared by a <code>$4030</code> read, a <code>$4023</code> write with bit 0 = 0, and nothing else; a <code>$4031</code> read, a disarming <code>$4022</code> write, a <code>$4025</code> write with bit 7 = 0 (with <code>FDS_BYTE_IRQ_GATE</code> = 1) and a taken <code>$4024</code> write (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 1) each drop the line and re-assert it while the flag is set.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example: $4023 bit 0 = 1, a disk set, the row counter at 126, the timer at 1000.\nCPU cycle k, an opcode fetch at $E123: the timer steps first (1000 -&gt; 999); then\n  the fetch, outside $6000-$DFFF, counts: rows 126 -&gt; 127.\nCPU cycle k+1, an operand read at $6200 (adapter RAM): timer 999 -&gt; 998; not counted.\nCPU cycle k+2, a write to $0300: timer 998 -&gt; 997; rows 127 -&gt; 0, so timer &lt;- 3245.\nCPU cycle k+3: timer 3245 -&gt; 3244.\nIf every CPU bus cycle from k+3 on were inside $6000-$DFFF, the timer would reach 0 at\ncycle k+3247 and, on cycle k+3248, become 4095 with the watchdog flag set and the line raised.</code></pre>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Measured on hardware, forum t=26359: \"7-bit refresh counter - reset to 0 while $4023.D0 = 0, increment on every CPU cycle spent accessing memory outside of PRG-RAM ... 12-bit CPU cycle timer - reset to all 1s (4095) while $4023.D0 = 0, reload with 3245 when refresh counter overflows, otherwise decrement on every CPU cycle. IRQ is asserted when the cycle timer underflows while $4023.D7 = 1\". Unresolved: another report gives bit 7 the opposite polarity; with bit 7 stuck at 1 that reading never fires. The row count is taken at the access, one cycle after the timer's step: a one-cycle placement on a 3,245-cycle timer. Failure mode without it: code that spins more than 3,245 cycles from adapter RAM with interrupts enabled never gets the interrupt (in the probe, 7 per frame vs 0).</p></div>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_FDS_HALT_REREAD</code></b> (30-APU, the DMC DMA). On a Disk System machine, each halted cycle of a DMC fetch that lands on a CPU read of <code>$4030</code> performs that read again, with every side effect above.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s5\">5. The timer interrupt</h4>\n<p><b>Confirmed by <code>fdsirqtests.fds</code> and <code>fdsirqtestsV7_patched.fds</code>, 21 sub-tests each, all passing with the <code>nintendo-rev1</code> drive firmware.</b></p>\n<p>State: a 16-bit reload value (<code>$4020</code>/<code>$4021</code>), a 16-bit counter, a repeat bit, an enable bit, and a pending flag.</p>\n<p>The timer is clocked <b>once per CPU cycle</b>, when the drive's clock runs (a disk is set), after the transport's step for that cycle. On each clock:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If not enabled, do nothing.</li><li>If the counter is 0: set the pending flag and assert the shared interrupt line. Then, if repeat is set, reload the counter from the reload value. Otherwise clear enable. This clock does not decrement.</li><li>Otherwise decrement the counter by 1.</li></ol>\n<p>Arming (<code>$4022</code> with bit 1 set) loads the counter from the reload value at the write. The interrupt therefore fires on the <b>(R + 1)-th clock</b> after the write, where R is the reload value. In repeat mode it fires every <b>R + 1</b> cycles after that. The reload value can be changed at any time. The change takes effect only at the next arming or the next repeat reload.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example: $4020 = $00, $4021 = $01 (R = 256), I/O enabled, $4022 = $03.\n  The write loads the counter with 256.\n  Clocks 1..256 count it down to 0; clock 257 fires: flag 1, line asserted,\n  counter reloaded to 256.  The next interrupt is at clock 514, then 771, ...\n  With $4022 = $02 instead (no repeat) it fires once at clock 257 and disarms.\n  R = 0 with repeat fires on every clock.</code></pre>\n<p>The timer is acknowledged and stopped as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>event</th><th>timer flag</th><th>enable</th><th>shared line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>read <code>$4030</code></td><td>cleared</td><td>unchanged</td><td>dropped</td></tr><tr><td>read <code>$4031</code></td><td><b>unchanged</b></td><td>unchanged</td><td>dropped, then re-asserted if the watchdog flag (4.8) is set</td></tr><tr><td>write <code>$4022</code> with bit 1 = 0 (and decode open)</td><td>cleared</td><td>cleared</td><td>dropped, then re-asserted if the watchdog flag (4.8) is set</td></tr><tr><td>write <code>$4023</code> with bit 0 = 0</td><td>cleared</td><td>cleared</td><td>dropped</td></tr><tr><td>write <code>$4025</code> with bit 7 = 0 (gate on)</td><td>unchanged</td><td>unchanged</td><td>dropped, then re-asserted if the timer flag or the watchdog flag (4.8) is set</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Reading <code>$4031</code> while the timer flag is pending (and the watchdog flag is not) drops the line without re-asserting it. The timer flag still reads 1 in <code>$4030</code> bit 0, but the CPU no longer sees an interrupt until the timer fires again. This is the core's behaviour and an implementation must match it (see Open questions).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s6\">6. The interrupt line</h4>\n<p>The timer and the byte transfer are <b>two sources on one line</b>. That line is the core's single cartridge-interrupt line, which the mapper chips also use (on mapper 20 nothing else drives it). It is separate from the frame counter's and the DMC's lines, and <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">the CPU chapter</a> specifies how the CPU polls the OR of them. The line is a <b>level</b>. Each source sets it, and the events in the table above (and the byte events below) release it.</p>\n<p><b>The byte-transfer source</b> asserts the line at the end of a byte period that delivers a byte (read) or frees the shift register (write), and only if <b>both</b> <code>$4025</code> bit 7 and <code>$4023</code> bit 0 are 1 at that moment. Setting bit 7 later, while byte-ready is already 1, does <b>not</b> raise the line. The line goes up only at a byte period.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>event</th><th>byte-ready</th><th>shared line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>byte period (read, outside gaps; or any write period)</td><td>set</td><td>asserted if <code>$4025</code> bit 7 and <code>$4023</code> bit 0</td></tr><tr><td>read <code>$4031</code></td><td>cleared</td><td>dropped, then re-asserted if the watchdog flag (4.8) is set</td></tr><tr><td>read <code>$4030</code></td><td>unchanged; cleared with <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 1, the default (4.8)</td><td>dropped</td></tr><tr><td>taken write to <code>$4024</code>, with <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 1 (the default)</td><td>cleared</td><td>dropped, then re-asserted if the timer flag or the watchdog flag is set</td></tr><tr><td>write <code>$4025</code> bit 7 = 0, with <code>FDS_BYTE_IRQ_GATE</code> = 1</td><td>unchanged</td><td>dropped, then re-asserted if the timer flag or the watchdog flag (4.8) is set</td></tr><tr><td><em>stop</em>, eject, insert (section 7.1)</td><td>cleared</td><td>unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>a soft reset</td><td>unchanged</td><td>dropped: the CPU's reset clears every interrupt line (the drive is not reset)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>FDS_BYTE_IRQ_GATE</code> = 1 is the rule above. The rival, 0, makes bit 7 a latch-style enable: a pending byte interrupt survives a write of bit 7 = 0.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> An enable gates a level, and a closed gate passes nothing (another emulator's source has the same rule in a wider form). Measured: without it, eight unlicensed disks that enable NMI part-way through the load take a stale transfer interrupt inside a firmware routine and fall back into the firmware. A second rival, drop the line on <em>every</em> <code>$4025</code> write, moved exactly the same disks when swept. The narrower rule is kept because it is the one with a reason.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s7\">7. The drive as a transport</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.1 State, power-on, and the two reset actions</h5>\n<p>The drive's state is: the media (buffer, size, side count, current side, inserted); the <code>$4025</code>/<code>$4023</code> control bits; the <b>head</b> (a byte offset into the current side, 0-65,500); the <b>byte timer</b>; the <b>gap</b> state (in-gap flag and gap bytes remaining); the <b>block</b> state (bytes of the current block remaining, CRC bytes remaining, the pending file size from the last file header, and a past-the-end flag); the write latch and write-protect; the delivered data byte; byte-ready; end-of-head; a scanning flag; and the spin-up counter.</p>\n<p><b>Power-on</b> (only at power-on; a soft reset leaves the drive untouched, so a title that resets to its own menu does not reload). First, every field is cleared to zero, except that the buffer pointer, its size and the side count survive. Then:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>inserted = 1 if a buffer is set; side = 0;</li><li>in-gap = 1, gap remaining = 0;</li><li>byte timer = <code>FDS_CYCLES_PER_BYTE</code>;</li><li>write-protect = <code>FDS_WRITE_PROTECT</code>;</li><li>motor 0, transfer reset 0, <b>direction 0 (write)</b>, I/O and sound disabled, timer disarmed, spin-up 0;</li><li><code>r4025</code> = 0; the watchdog's row counter = 0, its cycle timer = 0 and its flag = 0 (4.8 says what the first drive clock does to the timer);</li><li>the disk-identity record: count 0 and all ten bytes 0 (section 14);</li><li>(the wavetable's power-on contents: see <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a>).</li></ul>\n<p><em><b>Stop</b></em> (a <code>$4025</code> write with the motor bit 0, an eject, or an insert) does the following:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>scanning = 0;</li><li><b>head = 0</b>;</li><li>gap remaining = 0, in-gap = 1;</li><li>byte-ready = 0;</li><li>end-of-head = 0;</li><li>the block state is cleared: remaining 0, CRC 0, pending file size 0, past-the-end 0.</li></ul>\n<p><em>Stop</em> does <b>not</b> reset the byte timer or the spin-up counter.</p>\n<p><b>There is no seek.</b> The only ways back to the start of a side are <em>stop</em> and holding the transfer reset (the drive is a loop of tape-like media under a fixed head).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.2 The per-cycle step</h5>\n<p>The drive's four clocks run once per CPU cycle, in this order: the watchdog's cycle timer (4.8), transport, timer, wavetable. They run only while a disk buffer is set (section 3). They run after the mapper and expansion-sound clocks of the same cycle, and before the CPU access that ends the cycle. So a register access sees the drive <em>after</em> that cycle's step.</p>\n<p>The <b>transport step</b>:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Spin-up.</b> If the spin-up counter is above 0 <em>and</em> the motor is on <em>and</em> a disk is inserted, decrement it.</li><li><b>Hold.</b> If no disk is inserted, or the motor is off, or the transfer reset is set, or spin-up is above 0 (any one of the four is enough), the transport does not advance. In addition, whenever the transfer reset is set, whether or not a disk is inserted, the step holds the head at the start: head = 0, in-gap = 1, gap remaining = <code>FDS_GAP_BYTES</code>, byte-ready = 0, and the block state is cleared. End-of-head is <em>not</em> cleared by holding. The byte timer is frozen while held. Stop here.</li><li><b>Byte timer.</b> If the timer is above 0, decrement it and stop here. Otherwise reload it with <code>FDS_CYCLES_PER_BYTE</code> and run one <b>byte period</b>: 7.4 when reading, section 8 when writing (<code>FDS_WRITE_SUPPORT</code> = 1 and direction = 0).</li></ol>\n<p>So a byte period is <b><code>FDS_CYCLES_PER_BYTE</code> + 1 = 150 CPU cycles</b>. The timer is reloaded with 149 and the period runs on the clock where it is found at zero. That is about 83.8 µs per byte, or 11,932 bytes per second, at the NTSC CPU rate (measured on hardware: the drive's data rate is about 96.4 kbit/s, close to 149-150 cycles per 8-bit byte; the extra cycle is how the core counts, and an implementation must reproduce 150).</p>\n<p><em><b>Release</b></em> (transfer reset 1→0 at a <code>$4025</code> write) does the following:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>scanning = 1;</li><li>byte timer = <code>FDS_CYCLES_PER_BYTE</code>;</li><li>in-gap = 1, gap remaining = <code>FDS_GAP_BYTES</code> (the leader);</li><li>end-of-head = 0;</li><li>the block state is cleared.</li></ul>\n<p>The head was already held at 0.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.3 Motor, spin-up and ready</h5>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>Spin-up starts only on a motor <b>0→1</b> write. It lasts <code>FDS_SPINUP_CYCLES</code> = 200,000 CPU cycles, about 6.7 NTSC frames, counted only while the motor is on and a disk is in. While it runs, the transport is held and <code>$4032</code> bit 1 reads not-ready. When it reaches 0, the drive is ready in the same cycle.</li><li>Inserting a disk does <b>not</b> restart spin-up. If the motor is already on and spin-up has finished, an inserted disk is ready at once, unless transfer reset is held.</li><li><b>Ready while the transfer reset is held</b> (<code>FDS_READY_ON_RESET</code> = 1): <code>$4032</code> bit 1 reads not-ready while <code>$4025</code> bit 1 is set (taken from other emulators' source, where two agree; the reason, \"ready means the head is on the recorded area of an active scan\", is not established by measurement. Measured failure without it: the disk <em>Silviana</em> loads, then waits forever in its own code for not-ready, for 16,200 frames). The rival, 0, reports ready as soon as the media and motor allow, whatever the reset bit is. With the term, the test disk loads, both fdsirqtests rows pass, and the library loads more disks than without it.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why spin-up exists.</b> The firmware waits for the drive to go <b>from not-ready to ready</b>. If the drive reports ready the moment the motor bit is set, there is no transition to see. Measured: 1,896,192 polls of <code>$4032</code> in 900 frames, with the head still at byte 0. The value is <b>tuned</b> to \"long enough for a poll loop to observe it\", not derived. The real spin-up is an analogue property of a motor.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.4 One read byte period</h5>\n<p>When the head has reached the end of the side (head ≥ 65,500), set end-of-head = 1 and scanning = 0, and deliver nothing. This repeats every period until a <em>stop</em> or a <em>release</em>. End-of-head is <code>$4030</code> bit 6 (the head runs off the recorded area and there is no wrap).</p>\n<p>Otherwise the period depends on the state, tested in this order:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>state</th><th>what the period does</th><th>delivers?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>in a gap</b>, gap remaining &gt; 0</td><td>gap remaining −1, the data register ← <code>$00</code> (it is overwritten although nothing is delivered, so a <code>$4031</code> read after a gap period returns <code>$00</code>), head unchanged</td><td><b>no</b> (with <code>FDS_GAP_SILENT</code> = 1)</td></tr><tr><td><b>in a gap</b>, gap remaining = 0</td><td>leave the gap and <b>begin a block</b> at the head (7.5). Head unchanged; the data register unchanged</td><td><b>no</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>CRC</b> remaining &gt; 0</td><td>CRC remaining −1, data = <code>$00</code>, head unchanged. When it reaches 0, enter a gap of <code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code></td><td><b>yes</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>past the end</b></td><td>head +1</td><td><b>no</b></td></tr><tr><td>otherwise: <b>data</b></td><td>data = the container byte at (side × 65,500 + head), or <code>$00</code> if that is beyond the buffer. Head +1. If the block's remaining length is non-zero, decrement it. When it reaches 0, enter <code>FDS_CRC_BYTES</code> CRC bytes (or, if that is 0, a gap of <code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code>)</td><td><b>yes</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>\"Delivers\" means: set byte-ready, and assert the line if <code>$4025</code> bit 7 and <code>$4023</code> bit 0 are both 1. The data register (<code>$4031</code>) is simply overwritten. There is no overrun flag, and a byte the CPU did not collect is lost.</p>\n<p>A block whose remaining length is 0 when its data starts streams with no boundary. In practice, that only happens if the direction is switched back to read in the middle of a block. Bytes then run on until the head leaves the side.</p>\n<p>With the rival <code>FDS_GAP_SILENT</code> = 0, every gap period <em>with gap remaining &gt; 0</em> delivers its <code>$00</code> as data, with byte-ready and an interrupt. The period that leaves the gap stays silent. This rival reproduces a measured defect: the firmware's first byte was <code>$00</code> where block type <code>$01</code> belonged, it rejected the disk after two reads of <code>$4031</code>, and it went to its \"remove the disk\" loop. The default follows the hardware: over unwritten surface the drive raises no byte-ready.</p>\n<p>So a gap of N bytes is <b>N + 1 silent periods</b>. The leader is 3,538 silent periods, a block gap 123, and the CRC is 2 delivered periods with the head standing still.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.5 Beginning a block: the container's block structure</h5>\n<p>The container stores no lengths. A block's length comes from its own type byte, except that a file-data block's length was written in the file-header block before it. All lengths below include the type byte, and the size field is little-endian (the disk format).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>type byte at the head</th><th>block</th><th>length</th><th>also</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$01</code></td><td>disk information</td><td>56</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>$02</code></td><td>file amount</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>$03</code></td><td>file header</td><td>16</td><td>record the <b>pending file size</b> = the bytes at offsets 13 (low) and 14 (high) of this block. If offset 14 is beyond the buffer, record 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>$04</code></td><td>file data</td><td>1 + pending file size</td><td>the undeclared-data rule applies (7.6)</td></tr><tr><td>anything else</td><td>not a block</td><td>none</td><td>the written surface has ended: past-the-end = 1, remaining 0, except under the undeclared-data rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A byte position beyond the buffer reads as type <code>$00</code>.</p>\n<p>Once the blocks run out, everything that follows in the container is filler that was never on the disk. The head keeps moving so that end-of-head can still be reached, but nothing is delivered. <b>Failure mode of treating filler as a block:</b> the firmware is handed a stream of <code>$00</code> after the last file and reports a disk error on a good disk.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.6 Undeclared data after a block (<code>FDS_UNDECLARED_DATA</code>)</h5>\n<p>The drive does not know where a block ends. It streams the surface, and the CPU decides how many bytes to take. The file header's size field is what the <em>firmware</em> believes, not what was written. Three disks in the library carry data after a block that a transport without this rule cannot read: <em>Quick Hunter</em>, <em>Jingorou</em> and <em>Kosodate Gokko</em>.</p>\n<p>Let \"the rest of the side\" be from the block start to the side's end, or the buffer's end if that comes first.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>value</th><th>rule</th><th>measured</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>anything after the last parseable block is silence (past-the-end)</td><td>boots none of the three</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>a non-block type byte where a block should start begins <b>one block that runs to the end of the side</b>, if any byte from there to the side's end is non-zero. Otherwise past-the-end</td><td>boots <em>Quick Hunter</em> only</td></tr><tr><td><b>2 (default)</b></td><td>as 1, <b>and</b>: when a <code>$04</code> block's declared end is followed by a byte that is not <code>$01</code>-<code>$04</code>, and any byte from that point to the side's end is non-zero, the <code>$04</code> block itself <b>runs to the end of the side</b>. There is no CRC or gap at its declared end</td><td>boots all three</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In value 2, \"a <code>$04</code> block's declared end\" is the byte position block start + 1 + pending file size. The extension test is made <b>only when that position lies before the end of the rest of the side</b>. When it lies at or past that end, the block keeps its declared length (no extension, no test), and the head reaches the side's end (end-of-head, 7.4) before or as the length runs out.</p>\n<p>A side whose remainder is all zeros behaves the same under every value.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example (value 2): side 0, a $04 block starting at head 60,000 with pending\nfile size 5,600.  Declared end = 60,000 + 1 + 5,600 = 65,601, which is past the\nside's end (65,500): no test, no extension.  Data bytes are delivered from heads\n60,000 to 65,499 (5,500 bytes); the next period finds head = 65,500 and sets\nend-of-head.  The 101 declared bytes beyond the side are never delivered.</code></pre>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Tuned: the container cannot say which reading is right, and both agree with how the drive works. 2 is chosen over 1 because it boots two more disks, and that is the whole reason. What would decide it: a raw surface capture, with its own gaps, of any of the three.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.7 Where the gaps come from, and why these lengths</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>gap</th><th>length</th><th>basis and reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>leader before the first block</td><td><code>FDS_GAP_BYTES</code> = 3537 bytes (3,538 silent periods, 530,700 CPU cycles, about 17.8 frames)</td><td><b>hardware rule</b>: a real disk carries roughly 28,300 bits of unwritten leader, which is 3,537 bytes. <b>Measured</b> check: sweeping the leader shows a threshold between 3,200 and 3,400, then a plateau to 16,000 or more (<code>$4030</code> read 4 times, <code>$4031</code> 118 times at every value from 3,400 to 16,000). 3,537 sits inside the plateau. What the leader races: after release, the firmware spends about fifteen frames on other work before it arms the byte interrupt. A 200-byte leader has ended long before then, and the first byte handed over is from the middle of the disk-information block</td></tr><tr><td>before every later block</td><td><code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code> = 122 bytes (123 silent periods)</td><td><b>hardware rule</b>: about 976 bits of unwritten surface. It lands inside a measured plateau. Rival 0 removes the per-block gap. A sweep from 0 to 3,537 changes nothing by itself while the CRC bytes are missing: the CRC is what the firmware needs</td></tr><tr><td>CRC after every block</td><td><code>FDS_CRC_BYTES</code> = 2 bytes, value <code>$00</code>, delivered as data, head stands still</td><td><b>hardware rule</b> for the count: every block on a disk ends with a two-byte CRC, and the firmware reads it (after the last data byte it reads <code>$4030</code>, sets <code>$4025</code> bit 6, and reads on). The <b>value is a declared limit</b>: the real checksums were discarded, and <code>$4030</code> bit 4 is never set. Rival 0: the firmware takes the next block's first two bytes as the CRC, and every later block starts two bytes late</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example: the start of side 0, read mode, I/O enabled, byte interrupt\nenabled, motor already spun up.  Cycle 0 = the $4025 write that releases the\ntransfer reset.\n  periods    1 .. 3537   gap bytes, silent\n  period     3538        gap ends; block type at head 0 is $01 -&gt; length 56\n  periods    3539 .. 3594  56 data bytes (head 0..55), each with byte-ready\n                            -- the first at cycle 3539 x 150 = 530,850\n  periods    3595, 3596  two CRC bytes ($00), head stays at 56\n  periods    3597 .. 3718  122 gap bytes, silent\n  period     3719        gap ends; block at head 56 begins (type $02 -&gt; length 2)\n  period     3720        first byte of the file-amount block</code></pre>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s8\">8. Writing</h4>\n<p><code>FDS_WRITE_SUPPORT</code> = 1 (the default). The write path runs when the direction bit (<code>$4025</code> bit 2) is 0. On the surface a drive writes a gap, a start mark, the block and its checksum. The container stores only the block (it has no bytes for gaps, start marks or checksums), so the write path maps the firmware's phases onto it (the container definition):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The byte goes <b>from <code>$4024</code> to the media</b>, instead of from the media to <code>$4031</code>.</li><li><b>Every boundary is the firmware's</b>, read from <code>$4025</code> bits 6 and 4 (<code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 1). The write path counts no gap, block or checksum lengths at all.</li><li><b>Gap, start-mark and checksum periods are absorbed</b>: the CPU's byte is consumed, nothing is stored, and the head does not move.</li><li><b>The surface keeps moving</b> (<code>NES_R22_FDS_SURFACE</code> = 1). A write does not stop the disk. While the firmware writes gap, the old gap before the old block passes under the head. The write path keeps a signed <b>slip</b> count (in byte periods) and uses the read path's gap and CRC counts for the old surface. The <b>surface step</b>, run by each gap period (bit 6 clear) and each zero byte before the mark (bit 6 set, mark armed, <code>$4024</code> latch = 0), does exactly one of these, the first that applies:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>if CRC remaining &gt; 0 (a checksum the read path still owed): CRC remaining −1; if it reaches 0, in-gap = 1 and gap remaining = <code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code> (the old block gap begins);</li><li>else, if in-gap = 1 and gap remaining &gt; 0: gap remaining −1;</li><li>else: slip −1 (the new block is one period <b>late</b>).</li></ol>\n<p>The start mark (a non-zero latch while the mark is armed) adds what is left of the old gap as <b>early</b>: slip + gap remaining if in-gap = 1, slip unchanged if in-gap = 0. It changes neither in-gap, gap remaining nor CRC remaining. On the first checksum period the gap after the new block is set to 1 + <code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code> + slip (the \"1\" is the second checksum period; set to 0 if the sum is below 0) and slip ← 0; every later checksum period subtracts 1 from gap remaining while it is above 0. So the next old block arrives where it always was on the surface. The slip is set to 0 at three points: a change of direction to write; the first checksum period (after it is used); and a gap period (bit 6 clear) that finds the start mark not armed, before that period's surface step.</p></li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why (item 4).</b> A disk is a moving surface; this matches another emulator, which keeps the surface literally: on a title that rewrites a block, every later CPU write lands at that emulator's instant. Rival <code>NES_R22_FDS_SURFACE</code> = 0: the head is held for the write's gap and a full gap is restarted at the checksum, so the block after a rewrite arrives late by the write's gap timing; measured 3 byte periods on one title.</p></div>\n<p>The write path also keeps a <b>start-mark-armed</b> flag, a <b>checksum-in-progress</b> flag and the <b>recorded block start</b> (a head position). The recorded block start is set to the head at a change of direction to write and at a start mark, and is 0 at power-on.</p>\n<p><b>One write byte period</b> (<code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 1). This runs after the end-of-head test in 7.4, which applies to writing too. The cases are tested in order:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>state</th><th>effect</th><th>byte-ready and interrupt</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4025</code> bit 6 clear (bit 4 either value)</td><td><b>gap</b>: absorbed. If the start mark is not armed, slip ← 0. Run the surface step (item 4). Arm the start mark (the next byte is expected to be the mark) and clear the checksum-in-progress flag</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>bit 6 set, bit 4 set (start mark armed or not)</td><td><b>checksum</b>: absorbed. If the checksum-in-progress flag is 0 (the <b>first</b> checksum period since it was last cleared by a gap period, a change of direction to write, or power-on), set it to 1 and close the block that begins at the recorded block start, whether or not a start mark came: if the container byte at (side × 65,500 + recorded block start) is <code>$03</code> (a file header) and its byte 14 lies inside the buffer, the pending file size ← its bytes 13 and 14 (low, high); then the <b>read path resumes in a gap</b>: in-gap = 1, gap remaining = 1 + <code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code> + slip (0 if below 0) and slip ← 0 (item 4; with <code>NES_R22_FDS_SURFACE</code> = 0, gap remaining = <code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code>), block remaining 0, CRC remaining 0, past-the-end 0. If the flag is already 1: gap remaining −1 if it is above 0 (with <code>NES_R22_FDS_SURFACE</code> = 0, nothing)</td><td><b>no</b>: the shift register is fed by the checksum generator, not by <code>$4024</code></td></tr><tr><td>bit 6 set, bit 4 clear, start mark armed</td><td><b>still gap until the first non-zero byte</b>: absorbed. If the <code>$4024</code> latch is 0, run the surface step (item 4). If the latch is non-zero, it is the start mark: add the early slip (item 4), disarm the mark and set the recorded block start = the head. In-gap, gap remaining and CRC remaining are left as they are</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>otherwise (bit 6 set, bit 4 clear, mark not armed)</td><td><b>block byte</b>: <b>store</b> the <code>$4024</code> latch at (side × 65,500 + head), if the disk is not write-protected and the position is inside the buffer, marking the image dirty if the byte changed. Head +1. Clear past-the-end. In-gap and gap remaining are not touched</td><td>yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>\"Byte-ready and interrupt\" means: set byte-ready, and assert the line if <code>$4025</code> bit 7 and <code>$4023</code> bit 0 are both 1. Byte-ready means \"the shift register is free\", and on a write that is the drive asking for the next byte.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example (a checksum before any start mark): read mode, head 300 inside a gap\n(in-gap 1, gap remaining 40, CRC remaining 0).  The firmware writes $4025 = $21\n(write): slip 0, mark armed, checksum flag 0, recorded block start = 300.\n  Period 1, $4025 = $21 (bit 6 clear): gap; mark armed, so slip stays 0;\n    surface step: gap remaining 40 -&gt; 39.\n  The firmware then writes $F1 (bits 6 and 4 set) with no start mark.\n  Period 2: checksum, flag 0 -&gt; first: flag 1; the block at head 300 is closed\n    (if the byte at side x 65,500 + 300 is $03, the pending file size is taken\n    from it); in-gap 1, gap remaining = 1 + 122 + 0 = 123, slip 0.\n  Period 3: checksum, flag 1: gap remaining 123 -&gt; 122.\n  Nothing was stored and the head stayed at 300.</code></pre>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The bit assignment is taken from another emulator's source and the register notes (section 4.4); the mapping onto the container follows from the container definition; \"the start mark is the first non-zero byte\" follows from how a reader finds a block, by the first set bit after a gap, so zeros before the mark are gap. Measured: taking the first byte after bit 6 rises as the mark stores the firmware's <code>$80</code> as data and breaks block 2 at offset 56. The result matches another emulator: three titles' written images are byte-identical to another emulator's for the same play, below.</p></div>\n<p>Consequences an implementation must reproduce:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>The normal entry is from a read.</b> The firmware reads forward to the block before the one it will write, and then clears bit 2. The read path has just finished a checksum and is inside a gap. The direction change clears past-the-end and the block's remaining length and arms the start mark. The firmware then writes gap (bit 6 clear), raises bit 6, hands over zeros and the <code>$80</code> mark, and the write path stores from the head, which is the next block's first byte in the container.</li><li><b>A written file header sets the pending file size</b>, so a data block read straight after it has the length just written. The read path's own block-length rule (7.5) is otherwise unchanged.</li><li><b>Write protection is obeyed only at the store.</b> A protected disk still turns, still raises byte-ready, and still consumes <code>$4024</code>. Only the head is not energised. <em>Failure mode of refusing the transfer instead: the firmware hangs waiting for a period that never completes.</em></li><li><b>The firmware writes <code>$4024</code> while reading.</b> Measured: 46,678 writes in a boot that never leaves read mode. A core that acted on <code>$4024</code> without checking bit 2 would overwrite the disk it was loading. In this core <code>$4024</code> only fills the latch.</li><li><b>What is written back into the image buffer:</b> only the block bytes, at the head's container offset. That is the same buffer the host passed in, so the buffer <em>is</em> the save. The core keeps no second copy. <code>nes_fds_image</code> and <code>nes_fds_image_size</code> hand the host the buffer, and <code>nes_fds_dirty</code> reports whether any byte has changed. Saving it to storage is the host's job. The host's swap control (<code>FdsDrive</code> in <code>web/shell.js</code>) reports its own <code>dirty</code> as always false; <code>nes_fds_dirty</code> is the one to read.</li><li><b>Power-on</b> clears the write path's state (no mark armed, no checksum in progress, block start 0), with the rest of the drive (7.1).</li></ul>\n<p><b>The proof.</b> A save test plays three titles to their save and on into play: Metroid (Rev 2) stores 1,167 bytes, Zelda no Densetsu (Rev 1) 1,329, Link no Bouken (Rev 1) 845; each written image is <b>byte-identical</b> to the image another emulator wrote for the same play, so the result matches another emulator (the test checks a recorded hash of each image); each written image, reloaded after a fresh power-on, shows the registered name, and the block count, file amount and parse end of every side are unchanged; the write-protected control stores nothing (measured, 3 of 3 titles). Only the <code>nintendo-rev1</code> firmware was driven. Falsifier, run: <code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 0 stores 0 bytes on all three and the gate reds on 9 checks.</p>\n<p><b>Rival <code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 0</b>: bit 6 is read as the end of a block and bit 4 is ignored; the period cases are then (in a gap, gap remaining &gt; 0: −1, absorbed; gap remaining 0: leave the gap, absorbed; CRC remaining &gt; 0: −1, absorbed, entering a gap at 0; bit 6 set: block over, absorbed, start <code>FDS_CRC_BYTES</code> CRC periods; otherwise store), and every period raises byte-ready. Against the real firmware it stores nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Rival <code>FDS_WRITE_SUPPORT</code> = 0</b>: the read path runs in both directions, the <code>$4024</code> latch is never used, and a title's save silently does nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Write-protect.</b> <code>FDS_WRITE_PROTECT</code> = 0, which means writable (not established on hardware, a declared choice: a real disk is writable unless its tab is broken out, and a container carries no tab). The rival is 1, every disk protected. In that case a title that saves shows \"disk is write protected\", which is a different screen from the one a missing write path gives. The runtime control <code>nes_fds_set_write_protect</code> changes it after power-on. Power-on loads the define's value.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s9\">9. The drive firmware's role</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The core <b>does not patch, skip or accelerate</b> the drive firmware. It is mapped read-only at <code>$E000</code>, it supplies the reset, NMI and IRQ vectors, and it runs as ordinary CPU code. There is no \"fast load\" path.</li><li>The core knows <b>exactly one</b> firmware address: <code>$E445</code>, the entry point of the firmware's disk-identity check. It <b>observes</b> the opcode fetch there and records which disk the program asked for (section 14). That observation changes no register, no cycle, no bus value and no flag: a core with it and a core without it run every title identically, and only the two exports <code>nes_fds_want_count</code> and <code>nes_fds_want</code> differ. Apart from it there is no hook on any firmware address and no knowledge of the firmware's routines anywhere in the core.</li><li>Everything the firmware does (detecting the disk, spinning the motor, waiting for ready, releasing the transfer reset, arming the byte interrupt, reading the blocks, checking the license screen, loading files into adapter RAM and character RAM, and handing over to the program) happens through the registers above. That is why the transport's timing matters: <b>the firmware times the drive</b>. A model that returns bytes instantly passes a simple loader and fails this firmware.</li><li>Results can differ between firmware revisions. Nine disks behaved differently on <code>nintendo-rev1</code> and <code>nintendo-orig</code>, and eight of those nine were the stale byte interrupt that <code>FDS_BYTE_IRQ_GATE</code> removes (measured).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s10\">10. Disks: insert, eject, side swap</h4>\n<p><b>Exports</b> (the full signatures are in <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">the ABI chapter</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>export</th><th>behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>nes_set_disk</code></td><td>sets the buffer pointer and the size exactly as passed (a null pointer included), side count = size ÷ 65,500 (integer division, then only its low 8 bits are kept, so 256 sides read as 0), and inserted = 1 if the pointer is non-null, 0 if it is null. Call it <b>before</b> <code>nes_power_on</code>. With a null pointer no drive clock runs and no row is counted (section 3), <code>nes_fds_insert</code> does nothing, and power-on leaves inserted = 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_eject</code></td><td>inserted = 0, then <em>stop</em>. From this moment <code>$4032</code> reads no-disk, not-ready and protected</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_insert</code></td><td>if no buffer is set, nothing happens. Otherwise side = the argument if it is less than the side count, else 0; inserted = 1; then <em>stop</em>. It does not restart spin-up</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_sides</code></td><td>the side count</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_side</code></td><td>the current side</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_inserted</code></td><td>1 if a disk is in the drive</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_head</code></td><td>the head's offset in the current side</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_state</code></td><td>the packed snapshot below</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_writes</code></td><td>diagnostic: CPU writes seen at <code>$4020</code>-<code>$4026</code>, including writes a decode gate (4.1, 4.8) dropped; the adapter's own <code>$4025</code> = <code>$06</code> write (4.8) is not counted</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_reads</code></td><td>diagnostic: reads of <code>$4030</code>+(index AND 3)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_last4025</code></td><td>diagnostic: the last value the CPU wrote to <code>$4025</code>, recorded even if the decode gate dropped the write; the adapter's own <code>$06</code> write does not change it</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_last_data</code></td><td>diagnostic: the last byte returned by a <code>$4031</code> read</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_last_data_head</code></td><td>diagnostic: the head position at that read</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_bytes_written</code></td><td>diagnostic: bytes stored into the buffer</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_bytes_absorbed</code></td><td>diagnostic: write-mode periods absorbed (gap, start mark, checksum)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_write_periods</code></td><td>diagnostic: byte periods spent in write mode</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_w4024</code></td><td>diagnostic: taken writes to <code>$4024</code>. A write the <code>$4023</code> gate drops (4.8) is <b>not</b> counted here (it is counted by <code>nes_fds_writes</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_dirty</code></td><td>1 once a store has changed any byte of the buffer (with <code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 0: once any byte has been stored)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_write_protect</code></td><td>the current write-protect state</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_set_write_protect</code></td><td>sets it (non-zero = protected)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_tick</code></td><td><b>harness only</b>: one transport step, with no timer, CPU, picture or audio</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_data_peek</code></td><td>the data register, without the <code>$4031</code> side effect</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_byte_ready</code></td><td>byte-ready, without side effect</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_in_gap</code></td><td>the in-gap flag</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_image</code></td><td>the buffer pointer (the save)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_image_size</code></td><td>its size</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_want_count</code></td><td>how many disk-identity checks have started since power-on (section 14)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_fds_want</code></td><td>byte <em>index</em> (0 to 9) of the disk identity recorded at the latest check; 0 for an index above 9 (section 14)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The <code>nes_fds_state</code> bit layout.</b> This is a diagnostic snapshot and drives no behaviour.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>field</th><th>source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>motor</td><td><code>$4025</code> bit 0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>transfer reset</td><td><code>$4025</code> bit 1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>direction (1 = read)</td><td><code>$4025</code> bit 2</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>disk I/O enable</td><td><code>$4023</code> bit 0</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>sound enable</td><td><code>$4023</code> bit 1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>timer enable</td><td><code>$4022</code> bit 1, cleared by a one-shot fire</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>byte-interrupt enable</td><td><code>$4025</code> bit 7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>byte-ready</td><td><code>$4030</code> bit 1</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>end of head</td><td><code>$4030</code> bit 6</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>scanning</td><td>1 from <em>release</em> until <em>stop</em> or end-of-head</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>inserted</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>11-31</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Why a swap is two events.</b> The firmware learns that a disk arrived by watching <code>$4032</code> go not-ready and then ready again. An instant side change gives it no transition to see. <code>nes_fds_insert</code> while a disk is already in the drive rewinds the head (<em>stop</em>), but <code>$4032</code> never reads no-disk. <b>Selecting a side while a disk is in the drive is not a swap.</b> Several titles ship on more than one disk side and stall at the swap prompt forever without a real eject (for example <em>Shin Onigashima</em>, which comes in two parts).</p>\n<p><b>The host's swap policy</b> (<code>web/shell.js</code> <code>FdsDrive</code>; an implementer receives it):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>action</th><th>constant</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>first insertion (<code>boot</code>)</td><td>eject immediately after power-on, run 8 frames, insert side 0</td><td><code>FDS_FIRST_INSERT_FRAMES</code> = 8</td><td>tuned. Starting with a disk already in gives the firmware no edge, measured at 877,038 polls of <code>$4032</code> in 600 frames with the head at byte 0</td></tr><tr><td>swap (<code>swapTo</code>, <code>next</code>)</td><td>eject, run 60 frames, insert the requested side. <code>next</code> goes to (side + 1) modulo the side count</td><td><code>FDS_SWAP_GAP_FRAMES</code> = 60</td><td>tuned: it made the swap work. Nobody has measured the minimum and no test scores it</td></tr><tr><td>hold out (<code>eject</code>)</td><td>eject and leave it out</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The host owns the frame pump and the timing. The core owns only the instantaneous events.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s11\">11. Region</h4>\n<p>A disk carries no region. The drive and its firmware are Japanese, so the host runs a disk as <b>NTSC</b> unless the caller explicitly passes another region. All drive figures in this chapter are CPU-cycle counts tied to the NTSC rate. The core does not rescale them for another region.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s12\">12. Registered choices</h4>\n<p>Each choice is a build-time define; the default is the value shown.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>choice</th><th>default</th><th>meaning</th><th>rival</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>FDS_CYCLES_PER_BYTE</code> = 149</td><td>149</td><td>byte timer reload; a byte period is this + 1 = 150 CPU cycles</td><td>any other value (a sweep knob)</td><td>hardware measurement (data rate)</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_READY_POLARITY</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td><code>$4032</code> bit 1 = 1 means <b>not</b> ready</td><td>0: bit 1 = ready (the transfer-reset term is not inverted)</td><td>hardware measurement: public register documentation gives bit 1 as \"not ready\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_SPINUP_CYCLES</code> = 200000</td><td>200000</td><td>CPU cycles of not-ready after a motor 0→1</td><td>0: no spin-up, so the firmware sees no edge (measured hang)</td><td>tuned</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_GAP_SILENT</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td>gap periods deliver nothing</td><td>0: each gap byte is delivered as <code>$00</code> with an interrupt (measured rejection)</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_GAP_BYTES</code> = 3537</td><td>3537</td><td>leader before the first block, in bytes</td><td>any value in the measured plateau (3,400 to over 16,000) behaves the same; 200 or below 3,200 fails</td><td>hardware rule (derived), plateau measured</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_BLOCK_GAP_BYTES</code> = 122</td><td>122</td><td>gap before every later block, in bytes</td><td>0: no per-block gap</td><td>hardware rule (derived)</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_CRC_BYTES</code> = 2</td><td>2</td><td>synthesised CRC bytes after each block, value <code>$00</code>, delivered as data</td><td>0: no CRC; later blocks start two bytes late</td><td>hardware rule (count); value is a declared limit</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_WRITE_SUPPORT</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td>direction bit 0 writes <code>$4024</code> into the buffer</td><td>0: the read path runs in both directions</td><td>hardware rule; round trip measured</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td><code>$4025</code> bit 6 is the transfer start and bit 4 the checksum control; the write path takes gap, start mark, block and checksum from them (section 8)</td><td>0: bit 6 ends a block, bit 4 ignored (stores nothing against the real firmware)</td><td>another emulator's source (bits); hardware rule (container mapping); matches another emulator (three titles' images)</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_WRITE_PROTECT</code> = 0</td><td>0</td><td>power-on write-protect state: writable</td><td>1: every disk protected</td><td>not established (declared)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R22_FDS_SURFACE</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td>the surface moves under the head while the drive writes; the gap after a written block is the old gap moved by the write's slip (section 8, item 4)</td><td>0: the head is held for gap and checksum periods and a full gap restarts at the checksum</td><td>hardware rule; matches another emulator (write timing on one title)</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_BYTE_IRQ_GATE</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td>writing <code>$4025</code> bit 7 = 0 drops the byte interrupt</td><td>0: a pending byte interrupt survives; or wider: drop on every <code>$4025</code> write</td><td>hardware rule; another emulator's source agrees</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_READY_ON_RESET</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td>not-ready while <code>$4025</code> bit 1 is held</td><td>0: ready ignores the transfer reset</td><td>another emulator's source; the reason is not established</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_UNDECLARED_DATA</code> = 2</td><td>2</td><td>non-zero bytes after a <code>$04</code> block's declared end extend it to the side's end; a non-block type byte with non-zero data after it is one block to the end</td><td>0: silence; 1: only the second half</td><td>tuned</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_IO_DECODE_GATE</code> = 1</td><td>1</td><td><code>$4022</code> writes are dropped while <code>$4023</code> bit 0 is clear; <code>$4020</code>/<code>$4021</code> are not gated</td><td>0: <code>$4022</code> always decoded (two fdsirqtests sub-tests fail)</td><td>test: fdsirqtests</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_SIDE_BYTES</code> = 65500</td><td>65500</td><td>bytes per side in the container; not overridable</td><td>none</td><td>hardware rule (container definition)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s13\">13. Not established, and open questions</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b><code>$4025</code> bits 4 and 6</b> are specified in section 8 (<code>FDS_WRITE_BITS</code>). Open: the write path was driven only with the <code>nintendo-rev1</code> firmware, and <code>$4030</code> bit 4 (checksum error) is never set.</li><li><b><code>$4030</code> side effects.</b> With <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 0 the core clears only the timer flag at a <code>$4030</code> read and byte-ready survives until <code>$4031</code>; with the default 1 the read clears byte-ready as well (4.8). No test decides the byte-ready half.</li><li><b>A <code>$4031</code> read drops a pending timer interrupt's line</b> without re-asserting it, unlike a <code>$4025</code> bit 7 = 0 write. <code>$4023</code> disarms and <code>$4030</code> reads also drop the shared line unconditionally; a <code>$4022</code> disarm re-asserts it only for a set watchdog flag (4.8). That drop can hide a pending byte interrupt, but a byte interrupt is never re-raised by state alone. No test measures either case.</li><li><b>Writing <code>$4024</code> clears byte-ready</b> only with <code>NES_R18_FDS_BYTE_FLAG_D7</code> = 1 (the default, following the wiki). No test measures it.</li><li><b><code>$4024</code>-<code>$4026</code> and the I/O decode.</b> They are gated by <code>$4023</code> bit 0 only with <code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code> = 1 (the default, following the forum report). Not established by a test cartridge; no test covers them.</li><li><b><code>$4030</code> bit 7</b> is constant 1 and <b><code>$4033</code></b> reports battery good (bit 7) and open bus below it, both with their 4.8 switches at 0; the defaults make them byte-ready and the motor state (4.8).</li><li><b>Spin-up length, first-insert delay and swap gap</b> are tuned. None has a measured minimum.</li><li><b>What the <code>fdsirqtestsV7_patched</code> build changes</b> compared with the unpatched one is not established. Their 21 verdicts match on this core.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s14\">14. The disk-identity check</h4>\n<p>A Disk System program never names a disk side in a register. Before a load it puts a ten-byte <b>disk identity</b> in memory, stores that identity's address in zero page <code>$00</code> (low) and <code>$01</code> (high), and calls the drive firmware's <b>disk-identity check</b> at <code>$E445</code>. The check compares the side in the drive with that identity byte by byte, <code>$FF</code> matching any byte; a mismatch is the error the title then shows (\"SET SIDE B\", \"A,B SIDE ERR.07\"). The core records the identity at the moment the check starts, so a host can tell which disk the program wants. This is the one firmware address the core knows (section 9).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.1 The record</h5>\n<p>The core keeps two fields for the check, outside every other Disk System behaviour:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>field</th><th>size</th><th>power-on value</th><th>changed by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>check count</td><td>32 bits, unsigned, wrapping from 4,294,967,295 to 0</td><td>0</td><td>+1 at the end of each recorded check (14.2 step 5)</td></tr><tr><td>identity</td><td>10 bytes, index 0 to 9</td><td>all 0</td><td>overwritten as a whole at each recorded check (14.2 step 4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Both fields are cleared only by power-on (7.1). A soft reset, <code>nes_set_disk</code>, <code>nes_fds_insert</code>, <code>nes_fds_eject</code> and every register access leave them unchanged.</li><li>Both fields are part of the machine context, so a save state saves and restores them (<a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a>).</li><li><code>nes_fds_want_count</code> returns the check count. <code>nes_fds_want(index)</code> returns identity byte <em>index</em> for an index from 0 to 9, and 0 for any larger index.</li><li>Neither field is read by the machine. Recording a check changes no register, flag, counter, cycle count, bus value or open-bus value; only these two exports differ between a core that records checks and one that does not.</li></ol>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.2 When a check is recorded, step by step</h5>\n<p>A check is recorded on a <b>read of CPU address <code>$E445</code> while the CPU's opcode-fetch flag is set</b>. The opcode-fetch flag (the SYNC flag of <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a>) is set for exactly two kinds of read: the first read of every instruction (its opcode fetch, BRK's included), and the first, discarded read of a hardware interrupt sequence (NMI or IRQ). It is clear on every other cycle, including the reset sequence and every operand, dummy, stack and vector read. The steps, for every read of the CPU bus:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If the cartridge's mapper number is not 20, record nothing. (A disk buffer need not be set: the check is keyed to the mapper number, section 3.)</li><li>If the address is not <code>$E445</code>, record nothing. Only the address <code>$E445</code> itself counts.</li><li>If the opcode-fetch flag is clear, record nothing. A data read of <code>$E445</code> (for example <code>LDA $E445</code>, or the operand reads of <code>JSR $E445</code>, which read the bytes <code>$45</code> and <code>$E4</code> at the instruction's own address) is not a check.</li><li>Otherwise, before the read returns its byte:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Let P = (the byte at internal RAM <code>$0000</code>) + 256 × (the byte at internal RAM <code>$0001</code>).</li><li>For i = 0 to 9 in order: identity byte i ← the <b>peek</b> of address (P + i) modulo 65,536. A peek has no side effects and returns: <code>$0000</code>-<code>$1FFF</code>, internal RAM at (address AND <code>$7FF</code>); <code>$2000</code>-<code>$401F</code>, the open-bus value; <code>$4020</code>-<code>$4092</code>, the open-bus value (no register is read, section 3); <code>$4093</code>-<code>$5FFF</code>, the open-bus value; <code>$6000</code>-<code>$DFFF</code>, adapter RAM; <code>$E000</code>-<code>$FFFF</code>, drive firmware. The open-bus value is the one left by the bus cycle before this read: this read's own byte is not yet on the bus. During the ten peeks the opcode-fetch flag is held clear, so a peek of <code>$E445</code> is not a second check.</li></ol></li><li>Check count ← check count + 1.</li><li>The read then completes as any other read of <code>$E445</code> does (section 3: the firmware byte at offset <code>$445</code>).</li></ol>\n<p>The rule covers every read of <code>$E445</code> made during an opcode-fetch cycle, whoever makes it. Besides the CPU's own fetch, two direct memory accesses can make one, because the opcode-fetch flag stays set while they run inside that cycle:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>a <b>sprite DMA</b> whose page is <code>$E4</code>, started by the opcode fetch that follows a <code>$4014</code> write (<a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a>): its read of <code>$E445</code> is recorded;</li><li>a <b>DMC fetch</b> from address <code>$E445</code> that runs inside an opcode-fetch cycle: its read is recorded.</li></ul>\n<p>When such a DMA is followed by the CPU's own opcode fetch at <code>$E445</code> in the same cycle, two checks are recorded, the DMA's first.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>Worked example 1: a title's load call.\n  Internal RAM $00 = $00, $01 = $03.  The title has stored its identity at\n  $0300-$0309:  $01 $41 $42 $43 $20 $00 $01 $00 $FF $FF.\n  Check count before: 4.  The title runs JSR $E445 from adapter RAM at $6120.\n  JSR cycle 1: opcode fetch at $6120 (flag set, address not $E445): nothing.\n  JSR cycles 2 and 6: operand reads at $6121, $6122 (flag clear): nothing.\n  JSR cycles 3-5: stack and dummy accesses: nothing.\n  Next cycle: the opcode fetch at $E445 (flag set): P = $0300;\n    identity &lt;- $01 $41 $42 $43 $20 $00 $01 $00 $FF $FF; count 4 -&gt; 5.\n  nes_fds_want_count returns 5; nes_fds_want(6) returns $01; nes_fds_want(10) returns 0.\n\nWorked example 2: what is and is not a check.\n  LDA $E445                          not a check (data read).\n  JMP $E445, then its opcode fetch   one check, at the fetch.\n  An NMI is taken with PC = $E445:   one check at the interrupt's discarded\n    fetch of $E445 (identity from $00/$01 at that moment), and one more when\n    RTI returns there and the real opcode fetch runs: two in all.\n  RAM $00 = $40, $01 = $E4, so P = $E440: the peek of $E445 (i = 5) returns the\n    firmware byte and is not counted as a check.\n  P = $FFFA: bytes 0-5 come from $FFFA-$FFFF (firmware), bytes 6-9 from\n    $0000-$0003 (internal RAM): the address wraps modulo 65,536.\n  P = $4030: bytes 0-2 are the open-bus value; $4030's side effects do not happen.</code></pre>\n<p>Measured on the three pinned drive firmware images (section 1): the firmware's own check at boot asks for side 0 of disk 0 with every other byte <code>$FF</code> (identity bytes 6 and 7 = <code>$00</code>, all others <code>$FF</code>); a title's own load names that title's identity.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Basis: mechanism. <code>$E445</code> is the drive firmware's published entry point for its disk-identity check. Titles call it at that fixed address, directly and through the firmware's load-files entry at <code>$E1F8</code>, which is why every drive firmware a title can run on keeps it there. Recording on the opcode fetch only (the opcode-fetch flag) is what makes a call different from a data read: without that test, a title that copies the firmware would be recorded as asking. The ten identity bytes are the same ten bytes that follow the 14-byte check text in a side's disk-information block (block offsets 15 to 24): maker code (identity byte 0), game name (bytes 1-3), game type (byte 4), version (byte 5), side number (byte 6), disk number (byte 7), disk type (byte 8) and the byte after it (byte 9). The host compares the recorded identity with those offsets of every side of the mounted image, <code>$FF</code> matching any byte, to choose a side to insert; the core does no swapping. A title with its own loader in RAM never calls <code>$E445</code> (the measured cases are <em>Monitor Puzzle</em> and the <em>Fruits Mahjong</em> engine), so its wish is not recorded and the host must infer it from the drive.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-50-fds-s15\">15. Glossary</h4>\n<p>Each term is used in this chapter only in the sense given here. Terms of the CPU, the picture unit and the cartridge are defined in their own chapters' glossaries.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>term</th><th>meaning in this chapter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>absorbed</td><td>of a write byte period: the CPU's <code>$4024</code> byte is consumed and nothing is stored (section 8)</td></tr><tr><td>adapter</td><td>the RAM adapter in the cartridge slot: 32 KB adapter RAM, 8 KB character RAM, the 8 KB drive firmware window and the registers at <code>$4020</code>-<code>$4092</code></td></tr><tr><td>adapter RAM</td><td>the 32 KB at <code>$6000</code>-<code>$DFFF</code> (section 3)</td></tr><tr><td>block</td><td>one unit of recorded data on a side: a type byte and its contents (7.5); on the surface it is followed by two CRC bytes and a gap</td></tr><tr><td>block start (recorded)</td><td>the head position the write path takes as the start of the block being written (section 8)</td></tr><tr><td>byte period</td><td>one step of the transport: <code>FDS_CYCLES_PER_BYTE</code> + 1 = 150 CPU cycles, in which at most one byte is delivered or taken (7.2)</td></tr><tr><td>byte-ready</td><td>the adapter's byte transfer flag: set when a byte period delivers a byte (read) or frees the shift register (write); <code>$4030</code> bit 7 with the defaults (4.8)</td></tr><tr><td>check count</td><td>the number of disk-identity checks recorded since power-on (14.1)</td></tr><tr><td>checksum period</td><td>a write byte period with <code>$4025</code> bits 6 and 4 both set (section 8)</td></tr><tr><td>container</td><td>the <code>.fds</code> file: sides of 65,500 bytes holding block contents only, with no gaps, CRCs or start marks</td></tr><tr><td>CRC bytes</td><td>the two bytes after each block on the surface; the read path delivers them as <code>$00</code> (7.4, 7.7)</td></tr><tr><td>decode gate</td><td>the rule that drops a register write while <code>$4023</code> bit 0 is 0 (<code>FDS_IO_DECODE_GATE</code> for <code>$4022</code>, <code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code> for <code>$4024</code>-<code>$4026</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>deliver</td><td>of a read byte period: overwrite the data register, set byte-ready, and assert the shared line if <code>$4025</code> bit 7 and <code>$4023</code> bit 0 are both 1 (7.4)</td></tr><tr><td>direction</td><td><code>$4025</code> bit 2: 1 = read, 0 = write</td></tr><tr><td>disk identity</td><td>the ten bytes a program hands the disk-identity check, <code>$FF</code> meaning \"any byte\" (section 14)</td></tr><tr><td>disk-identity check</td><td>the drive firmware's routine at <code>$E445</code> that compares the side in the drive with a disk identity; \"a check is recorded\" means 14.2 steps 4-5 ran</td></tr><tr><td>drive clock</td><td>the drive's per-CPU-cycle work: watchdog timer, transport, timer, wavetable, in that order (7.2); runs only while a disk buffer is set</td></tr><tr><td>drive firmware</td><td>the drive's 8 KB program, supplied by the user, mapped read-only at <code>$E000</code>-<code>$FFFF</code></td></tr><tr><td>early / late</td><td>of the write path's slip: the new block reaches the old surface position before (early, slip +) or after (late, slip −) the old block would have (section 8 item 4)</td></tr><tr><td>end-of-head</td><td>the flag set when the head reaches the side's end (head ≥ 65,500); <code>$4030</code> bit 6</td></tr><tr><td>gap</td><td>unwritten surface between blocks; on reading, gap periods deliver nothing (7.4)</td></tr><tr><td>gap remaining</td><td>the count of gap byte periods left before a block begins</td></tr><tr><td>head</td><td>the drive's position on the current side, a byte offset from 0 to 65,500</td></tr><tr><td>hold</td><td>the transport's state while the transfer reset is set: head at 0, leader reloaded (7.2 step 2)</td></tr><tr><td>in-gap</td><td>the flag that says the head is over a gap</td></tr><tr><td>leader</td><td>the gap before the first block of a side, <code>FDS_GAP_BYTES</code> bytes</td></tr><tr><td>opcode-fetch flag</td><td>the CPU's SYNC flag: set during an instruction's opcode fetch and a hardware interrupt's discarded first fetch (<a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>past-the-end</td><td>the flag that says no further block exists on the side; the head still moves but nothing is delivered (7.4, 7.5)</td></tr><tr><td>peek</td><td>a read of the CPU address space with no side effects and no cycle (14.2 step 4)</td></tr><tr><td>pending file size</td><td>the size taken from the last file-header block, used as the next file-data block's length (7.5)</td></tr><tr><td><code>r4025</code></td><td>the value of <code>$4025</code> as the adapter holds it: set by every taken <code>$4025</code> write, 0 at power-on (4.8)</td></tr><tr><td><em>release</em></td><td>the actions of a <code>$4025</code> write that changes the transfer reset from 1 to 0 (7.2)</td></tr><tr><td>row (refresh row)</td><td>one count of the watchdog's 7-bit refresh-row counter (4.8)</td></tr><tr><td>scanning</td><td>the flag set by <em>release</em> and cleared by <em>stop</em> or end-of-head</td></tr><tr><td>shared line</td><td>the core's single cartridge interrupt line, driven by the timer, the byte transfer and the watchdog (section 6)</td></tr><tr><td>side</td><td>65,500 bytes of the container; one face of a disk</td></tr><tr><td>slip</td><td>the write path's signed count of byte periods by which the new block is early or late against the old surface (section 8 item 4)</td></tr><tr><td>spin-up</td><td>the not-ready time after a motor 0→1 write, <code>FDS_SPINUP_CYCLES</code> CPU cycles (7.3)</td></tr><tr><td>start mark</td><td>the first non-zero <code>$4024</code> byte after <code>$4025</code> bit 6 rises in write mode; it begins a written block (section 8)</td></tr><tr><td><em>stop</em></td><td>the actions of a motor-off <code>$4025</code> write, an eject or an insert (7.1)</td></tr><tr><td>surface</td><td>the disk as the drive sees it: gaps, blocks and CRCs, rebuilt from the container as the head moves</td></tr><tr><td>surface step</td><td>the write path's step that lets the old surface pass under the head during a written gap (section 8 item 4)</td></tr><tr><td>taken</td><td>of a register write: not dropped by a decode gate</td></tr><tr><td>timer flag</td><td>the timer interrupt's pending flag, <code>$4030</code> bit 0 (section 5)</td></tr><tr><td>transfer reset</td><td><code>$4025</code> bit 1; while set the transport holds the head at the start of the side</td></tr><tr><td>transport</td><td>the drive's model as a moving head over a surface at a fixed byte rate (section 7)</td></tr><tr><td>watchdog flag</td><td>the DRAM watchdog's interrupt flag, <code>$4030</code> bit 1 with the defaults (4.8)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>";
function nesFdsEmulationTabHTML(d) {
  return '<div class="gbemu nesemu nesemu-fds">' + NES_EMU_FDS_HTML + '</div>';
}
